## Lintable structure

> Enforced deterministically by `tools/canon-lint/` on every push (`.github/workflows/canon-lint.yml`)
> and locally by `/canon-publish` before every push. Severities live in `lint/rules.yaml` (change via PR).
> The linter checks **internal consistency**; whether a fact matches reality is each agent's
> weekly `/canon-reconcile` — the LLM residual the linter deliberately leaves out.

### Folder schema — every `agents/<name>/`

```
agents/<name>/
  profile.md      REQUIRED — identity + index; the reachability root; always status: canonical
  facts.yaml      REQUIRED — the purely lintable zone: the claims other agents may rely on
  docs/           prose zone — explanations and models; front-matter envelope linted, body never read
  files/          shared artifacts (exports, specs, decks) — every file must be referenced somewhere
  projects/       shared (company) projects this agent manages — one `<slug>/` per project, see § Projects
  NEEDS-REVIEW.md reconcile's flag file — exempt from the schema
```

**The two-zone rule:** if the fleet may depend on it, it is an entry in `facts.yaml`; the doc is
the explanation behind it. Prose stays free — the linter never reads past a doc's front matter.

### `facts.yaml` — the purely lintable zone

```yaml
facts:
  - key: operating-model.review-cadence   # lowercase dotted subject.relation — the conflict grain
    value: "6 weeks — next review 2026-09-01"
    status: canonical                     # canonical | draft | superseded
    updated: 2026-07-23                   # last content change (YYYY-MM-DD)
    review_by: 2026-09-01                 # verification due date — past due on canonical = lint FAIL
    source: docs/operating-model.md       # local doc/file (must exist), URL, or an external ref
```

An empty zone is `facts: []`. A `key` has exactly **one home across all folders** (superseded
entries excepted) — two folders claiming the same key is the lint failure that kills silent drift.

### Doc envelope — `profile.md` and every `docs/*.md`

```yaml
---
owner: <agent-name>      # must equal the enclosing agents/<name>/ folder
status: canonical        # canonical | draft | superseded
updated: YYYY-MM-DD
review_by: YYYY-MM-DD    # verification due date; canonical + past due = lint FAIL
tldr: "One line — what this doc establishes"
---
```

**Statuses separate conviction levels** (ideas must not dress as canon):
- `canonical` — the fleet may rely on it; fully linted; must be linked from `profile.md`.
- `draft` — sandbox-in-place; legal anywhere in the folder, unlinted for staleness, and **must not**
  be linked from `profile.md`.
- `superseded` — kept for history; exempt from staleness and the one-home rule.

### Projects — `projects/<slug>/` (shared projects, ruling R21)

**One project-management standard, two visibility levels.** A project is managed the same
way whether it lives at agent level (`project_files/<slug>/` in the managing agent's own repo)
or at canon level (`agents/<owner>/projects/<slug>/` here): same `project.md` charter, same
registry epic, same steward, same intake path, same append-only decision ledger. Canon
placement decides **visibility only** — who can read the definition and rely on it. Company
projects are shared projects by design and live in canon.

```
agents/<name>/projects/<slug>/
  project.md      REQUIRED — the charter; front-matter envelope linted, body never read
  decisions.md    optional — append-only decision ledger; envelope linted, never re-stamped
  <anything else> the project's workspace — free-form and unlinted, exactly like project_files/<slug>/
```

Charter envelope — `project.md`:

```yaml
---
owner: <agent-name>                 # must equal the enclosing agents/<name>/ folder — the managing agent
status: active                      # active | blocked | needs-decision | paused | pending-verification | done
epic: <owner>/<repo>#<N>            # the registry epic this charter mirrors (the epic is the authoritative record)
updated: YYYY-MM-DD                 # last charter change
review_by: YYYY-MM-DD               # verification due date — past due (unless paused/done) = stale
tldr: "One line — what this project is for"
---
```

`status` mirrors the epic's `status:*` label (the PM standard's vocabulary, not the doc conviction
levels). `paused` and `done` charters are exempt from staleness; everything else past `review_by`
is a stale charter — `/canon-reconcile` verifies it against the epic (open/closed, status) and
re-stamps it.

Ledger envelope — `decisions.md`: `owner` (= folder) · `status` (`canonical | draft | superseded`)
· `updated` (last append) · `tldr`. **Append-only.** Nothing re-stamps a ledger — `updated:` moves
only when a decision is appended, and it carries no `review_by`; the charter's review covers it.

Read a shared project with `/canon-consume <agent> projects [slug]`; create one with
`/project-init --canon` (records the canon path in the epic body). Tandem —
`agents/corbin/projects/tandem/` — is the first shared project and the reference instance.

### Restricted grammar (why these files stay boring)

The lintable zone is a deliberate YAML subset so a zero-dependency linter can parse it exactly:
flat `key: value` scalars only, full-line comments only, values containing `": "` must be quoted.
Everything remains valid YAML for ordinary tools. Richer structure belongs in prose or `files/`.

### Lint rules

| Rule | What fails |
|---|---|
| `envelope` | missing/unparseable front matter, missing required keys, bad dates, bad status |
| `fact-schema` | `facts.yaml` missing or malformed, incomplete entries, bad dates/status |
| `key-grammar` | fact key not lowercase dotted `subject.relation` |
| `one-home-per-key` | same non-superseded key homed in two folders (or twice in one) |
| `ownership` | a file whose `owner:` differs from its enclosing folder |
| `staleness` | anything `canonical` past its `review_by` |
| `source-resolution` | a fact `source:` pointing at a local path that doesn't exist |
| `reachability` | canonical doc missing from `profile.md`'s index; draft linked; orphan in `files/` |
| `project-envelope` | `projects/<slug>/` without `project.md`; charter missing keys, bad `epic` shape (`owner/repo#N`), bad dates, status outside the epic vocabulary; `decisions.md` ledger envelope incomplete |
| `layout` (warn) | top-level entries outside the folder schema; a file directly under `projects/` |

**Migration note:** folders predating this section carry the v1 front matter
(`verified:`/`source:`, no `status:`/`review_by:`/`tldr:`). Port: `status: canonical`,
`review_by:` = old `verified:` + 30 days, `tldr:` from the doc's first heading; mirror
load-bearing claims into `facts.yaml`. The installer's migration seeding (a sanctioned
cross-folder write, like enrollment seeding) may scaffold `facts: []` and port envelopes —
the *content* of every fact stays the owner's to declare.
